hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse. Press C for the whole chapter.

=== Psalm 11
--- Psalm 11 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 11
sub: In the LORD put I my trust
refs: Psalm 11:1

--- Flee as a bird to your mountain?
kicker: Psalm 11 · In the Lord put I my trust
big: Psalm 11:1 | mark: In the LORD put I my trust; Flee as a bird to your mountain?
p: **Psalm 11** is a short song addressing a doubtful disputation of an unknown interlocutor, someone who may say our faith in the Lord is insufficient to save us from our enemies.
p: Our trust is in the Lord. We have no need to fear. To flee. Especially in the end times this Psalm's dual-fulfillment of prophecy, portends.
p: I think of all the fairweather Christians who spend their days in fear, prepping to flee to the mountains in the Tribulation. Who plan to flee as a bird to their mountain.

--- Privily shoot at the upright
kicker: Psalm 11 · The wicked bend their bow
big: Psalm 11:2 | mark: privily shoot at the upright in heart
p: The wicked seek to shoot their arrows at the hearts of the upright, even now. In the Tribulation, they won't need to do it privily, but will openly seek the lives of the children of God.

--- If the foundations be destroyed
kicker: Psalm 11 · The foundations
big: Psalm 11:3
p: David asks, rhetorically, *If the foundations be destroyed, what can the righteous do?*
p: If the Lord isn't true to save us, if the foundation of our faith is destroyed, we would of all men be most miserable.

--- If Christ be not risen
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 11:3
scripture: 1 Corinthians 15:13-17 | mark: if Christ be not raised, your faith is vain

--- Other foundation can no man lay
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 11:3
p: Because there is no other foundation upon which we may trust;
big: 1 Corinthians 3:11 | mark: which is Jesus Christ
p: Knowing this sure foundation, and knowing He is risen, we can place our trust in Him.

--- The Lord is in his holy temple
kicker: Psalm 11 · His throne is in heaven
big: Psalm 11:4 | mark: The LORD is in his holy temple, the LORD’s throne is in heaven
p: We know with certainty the Lord is in His holy Temple, the Lord is on His throne. We know He is the omnipotent judge of men.

--- The portion of their cup
kicker: Psalm 11 · The Lord trieth the righteous
scripture: Psalm 11:5-6 | mark: The LORD trieth the righteous; this shall be the portion of their cup
p: He tries, or tests the righteous to increase our faith. He hates the wicked and the violent, and shall rain down His wrath upon them. This is the portion of their cup. This is a promise of God.

--- The cup of his indignation
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 11:6
scripture: Revelation 14:10-11 | mark: the cup of his indignation

--- His countenance doth behold the upright
kicker: Psalm 11 · The righteous Lord
big: Psalm 11:7 | mark: his countenance doth behold the upright
p: But the righteous are before the face of the Lord, always.

--- All I see is Jesus
kicker: Psalm 11 · Reading the Psalms
p: I want to take the opportunity here, with such a short and concise Psalm, to deal with the way many modern "theologians" approach the Old Testament, and the Psalms in particular.
p: When I read and react to the text, all I see is Jesus, the gospel, and the clear and present application of worship in our lives, and the prophetic nature, which clearly is reflected in New Testament end times scripture.
p: Nearly every Psalm is an uplifting song of praise, worship and trust, with the warnings against the wicked and promises of deliverance from their evil, and God's wrath upon them.

--- Seeking to be wise
kicker: Psalm 11 · Reading the Psalms
p: Meanwhile, most studies become hyper fixated on the context David wrote them in, the situation he was facing. Often totally invented by the author (or parroting an earlier study of another man).
p: Seeking to be wise, they become fools. They want to impress the reader with how clever they are and how their "knowledge of the Hebrew" and extrabiblical sources puts them above the normal believer, who may read the plain text and see themselves, and the future promises of God, as the primary application today.

--- The forest for the trees
kicker: Psalm 11 · Reading the Psalms
p: [[say:typical|A typical example]];
p: These men miss the forest for the trees, and create this sterile and unrelatable version of the word, buried under jargon, and with little to no edification or doctrine.
p: I don't claim to ever exhaust the full meaning and layers of the text in my morning studies. God's word is deep and there are passages that carry a meaning and purpose today, and then read later, convey something totally different. Where the Spirit leads. But when a study becomes an exercise in dazzling the reader with theology, vain philosophy, jargon and concepts that come more from linguistics, semantics and traditions, than the Spirit, I recommend they be avoided.

=== Psalm 12
--- Psalm 12 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 12
sub: Help, LORD • The tongue • The pure words of the LORD
refs: Psalm 12:1

--- Help, Lord
kicker: Psalm 12 · Help
big: Psalm 12:1 | mark: Help, LORD
p: **Psalm 12** opens with the most plaintive cry, yet. Calling upon the Lord, "Help."
p: That simple prayer, asking for help during our trials, is perhaps the most often prayed.
p: "Help, Lord" because the circumstances have led to humble ourselves before Him. Our admission of weakness allows God to be our strength, and glorifies Him that deserves all glory.

--- Have you ever called out to the Lord?
kicker: Application • Soul winning
p: Even the erstwhile atheist, the fool who says in their heart, "there is no God," cries out for help from the deity they profess doesn't exist.
p: We often will ask someone we give the gospel to, have you ever called out to the Lord for help? Because of health, or the health of another. Cry out in fear, because of a crisis or an emergency need has left them no other way to turn, except towards the Lord and His mercy. Or something as simple as a job, or test. Nearly all will say "Yes" if they're being honest.

--- Have you called upon the Lord for salvation?
kicker: Application • Soul winning
p: From there, it becomes easier to lead them to the truth that they need to call upon the name of the Lord specifically to save them, eternally. To receive forgiveness of sin and everlasting life.
p: If man struggles with the things of this world on his own, how much more do we need help for the spiritual things of God?
p: Have you cried out to the Lord? Have you come before God, unable to go on, let alone articulate the need you are petitioning, beyond a simple "Help?"
p: Have you called upon the Lord for salvation?

--- Just Lot, vexed
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 12:1
p: David's plea for help, like many of the Psalms, comes from the vexation of spirit in dealing with the wicked of this world. That in a world where believers, Christians, are called to be separated and holy as He is holy, the fallen world full of the iniquity of man troubles us. It is the lot, no pun intended, of the believer.
scripture: 2 Peter 2:6-9 | mark: vexed with the filthy conversation of the wicked

--- In seeing and hearing
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 12:1
p: This is a feeling we can all relate to, if you walk in the Spirit.
p: The vexation is not just bearing witness to the unlawful deeds of the wicked, but in *hearing* them as well.

--- Flattering lips
kicker: Psalm 12 · The tongue
scripture: Psalm 12:2-3 | mark: with flattering lips and with a double heart do they speak
p: David says they speak vanity to each other, and flatter, and with a double-heart, speak lies. They pridefully boast. It is the wickedness of words that sees David cry out.

--- By thy words
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 12:2
scripture: Matthew 12:34-37 | mark: out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaketh; by thy words thou shalt be condemned
p: Jesus shows us that words are the window to the heart of a man. An evil heart brings forth evil words, and it is those words that condemn them.

--- That which cometh out of the mouth
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 12:2
scripture: Matthew 15:10-11 | mark: that which cometh out of the mouth, this defileth a man
p: This understanding, that it wasn't the ritual washings and dietary laws, cleaning the outside of the cup -but the heart, and what proceeds out from it through the mouth, that makes a man unclean, is often neglected by Christians, today. But one of the most repeated commands in scripture is to watch what you say.
p: Proverbs is full of advice to remain silent, to not speak like the fool or the froward. To speak truth in all things. To refrain from vain, useless speech.

--- Our lips are our own
kicker: Psalm 12 · Who is lord over us?
big: Psalm 12:4 | mark: our lips are our own: who is lord over us?
p: But the wicked refuse to hear God's word and follow His way. Men *Who have said, With our tongue will we prevail; our lips are our own: who is lord over us?*
p: Of course, by admitting they have power over their own tongue, they condemn themselves.

--- The tongue is a little member
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 12:3–4
p: James, the half-brother of Christ, writes in his epistle, about the importance of controlling your tongue.
scripture: James 3:2-5 | mark: the tongue is a little member

--- The tongue can no man tame
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 12:3–4
scripture: James 3:6-8 | mark: the tongue can no man tame

--- That tiny member
kicker: Application • The tongue
p: Controlling your speech will go far in controlling your body. We talk ourselves into a multitude of sins. We give cause for the enemies of Christ to blaspheme by our publicly spoken words, far more than our sins done in secret.
p: When we curse, profane and use vulgarity, when we lie and double-deal. When we oppress and offend others. When we puff up with vain words and flattering lips. We defile the whole body by that tiny member, the tongue.

--- Now will I arise, saith the Lord
kicker: Psalm 12 · The sighing of the needy
big: Psalm 12:5 | mark: now will I arise, saith the LORD
p: This is a promise from God Himself. These are His words.
scripture: 2 Peter 1:20-21 | mark: holy men of God spake as they were moved by the Holy Ghost
p: The psalmist was moved by the holy Ghost to pen the words of the Lord. His promises are our sure foundation. His word settled forever in Heaven. Jesus is the Word made flesh. The promise fulfilled.

--- His words are powerful
kicker: Psalm 12 · The word of God
p: Faith cometh by hearing the word of God. Those that hear and do the things the Lord says are built upon a rock. We live by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God. All scripture is given by inspiration of God. It is the source of all our knowledge and understanding. His precepts give us understanding. We are told to meditate in His word day and night. To bind His words upon our heart. His words are powerful;
scripture: Hebrews 1:1-3 | mark: upholding all things by the word of his power

--- The word of truth
kicker: Psalm 12 · The word of God
p: The word which we preach is the power to save men's souls to all who trust His word:
scripture: Ephesians 1:12-14 | mark: after that ye heard the word of truth, the gospel of your salvation

--- Pure words, preserved for ever
kicker: Psalm 12 · The words of the Lord
p: And so, the preservation of God's word for all generations is a divine role. God supernaturally has revealed His word to man in sundry times and diverse manners, and He keeps His word pure.
scripture: Psalm 12:6-7 | mark: The words of the LORD are pure words; thou shalt preserve them from this generation for ever
p: If man needs every word of God to live, as much as bread, man needs access to every word He has given us.

--- The every-word Bible
kicker: Application • The King James Bible
p: The blessing of having a consolidated, complete, incorruptible source, an every-word Bible, cannot be overstated. The King James Bible is the every-word source, in English, without error.
p: The 6 earlier English attempts, like a refiner's fire. We have the purified silver of the Authorized version. We can know the Lord has preserved His word. We can trust and build upon that sure foundation.

--- Yea, hath God said?
kicker: Application • The King James Bible
p: Speaking of the sure word, preserved, modern versions change and corrupt this text. Rather than the promise to keep His words for all generations, they make it about preserving man;
quote: You, O Lord, will keep them; you will guard us[a] from this generation forever. | The ESV
p: The footnotes cast doubt. The changes remove the Psalm's comparison of the vain words of the wicked with certainty of God's word of promise.

--- Or God is a liar
kicker: Application • The King James Bible
p: The Lord promises to hear the sighing of the needy, and to arise to set them that call upon Him in safety. It is that promise that is preserved. And must be, or God is a liar.
p: The first thing modern versions do is create doubt and deny the preservation of the inspired word. Just as Satan tempted Eve in the garden, asking "Yea, hath God said?"

--- When the vilest men are exalted
kicker: Psalm 12 · The wicked walk on every side
big: Psalm 12:8
p: But the hope is in the help of the Lord, promised to us before the foundation of the world.

